  "findings": {
    "request": "The sCardFunc table points to the unnamed read callback. In the nonzero handle+0x1278 branch, the callback requests N+16 from FileX. When FileX returns success, the original code queues BurnHashInit, then BurnHashUpdate with actual_read-16 and BurnHashFanalize with destination+actual_read-16. Tested successful reads deliver all 80 bytes for N=64.",
    "queues": "BurnHashInit/Update/Fanalize enqueue three-word requests with codes 0, 1, and 2 at handle+0x127c. The manager dispatches these to md5_init, md5_append and md5_finish. The original memcmp compares 16 bytes. It sends code 3 on equality and 4 on mismatch to handle+0x12b4, retaining the expected-digest pointer in the second word. Fanalize blocks on the reply queue and returns 1 iff the received first word equals 3.",
    "retry": "Callback success returns 1 after freeing temporary RAM. Mismatch takes FileX relative seek with the original actual-read count, high word 0 and option 3, then can retry once. Two mismatches request seek twice before returning 0. A failed read or failed seek returns 0. The replay exercises first- and last-byte mismatches, a successful retry including changed synthetic payload, failed retry, initial read error, and read/seek errors after mismatch.",
    "modeling": "Each callback result receive invokes an isolated native worker using the exact captured request words and copies of the actual destination and handle RAM at the same virtual addresses. After the original native compare and reply, a fixture stop-5 message ends the worker; the stop reply stays in the harness. Only the worker's actual reply 3/4 is delivered to the original blocking wrapper. This models serialized delivery and a fresh worker invocation per transaction."
  },
